{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\nwc -l collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\nnl -ba collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,360p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\nwc -l collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\nnl -ba collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,360p'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n     330 collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n     1\t---\n     2\t\n     3\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-08 15:04 GMT\n     4\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-08 22:04 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     5\tCoding-Agent: claude (Opus 5)\n     6\tSession-ID: 0dae45ea-4204-49d9-bf35-c56e24cce14e\n     7\t\n     8\t# A complete-retention DaoStream v2 transport (r3)\n     9\t\n    10\tr2's six findings are confirmed closed. This revision changes `run-retention-laws` only, and folds in two rulings as prose. The correction is the same species as the P0 that started this work: a law generalized from one instance, quietly encoding that instance's properties. r2's law appended 256 values and compared the replay against all 256 — which would have failed a conforming capacity-2 complete-history writer after its second append, exactly the transport §3 argues is legitimate. Everything else stands.\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. The scoping judgement: confirmed\n    13\t\n    14\tVerified against the tree, not taken on report.\n    15\t\n    16\t- v1 local streams are opened bare: `(ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer})` with no `:capacity` — `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:219` and fifteen further sites, `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:569`, `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:103`. `dao.space` itself never creates one; the transactor takes it as `:local-stream`, \"supplied, never created, registered, or closed\" (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:8-14`).\n    17\t- v1 eviction is guarded by `(and capacity …)` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:76-91`), so a nil capacity short-circuits the branch: `:head` never advances, nothing is evicted, `:full` is unreachable and `:daostream/gap` is unreachable.\n    18\t- Nothing persists that stream. `publish-index!` (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:540-604`) snapshots it from position 0 (`snapshot-datoms`, `:442-468`) and emits b-tree blobs to a **v2** DaoJing intake stream; durability is entirely on the jing side. The code already says why complete retention is load-bearing (`index.cljc:561-564`). `derive-next-t` (`transactor.cljc:125-139`) and `schema/proposed-schema` (`schema.cljc:926`) rescan from zero for the same reason.\n    19\t\n    20\tSo the local stream is in-memory, unbounded, append-only, and dies with the process; a restarted process today derives `next-t` = 0 from an empty stream. **The faithful v2 counterpart is an in-memory, unbounded, append-only log.** A file-backed durable log would add semantics `dao.space` does not have and has not specified — it would silently change what a restart means — and durability across restarts is `dao.jing`'s job, which `dao.space` already composes with for publication.\n    21\t\n    22\tWhat `dao.space` actually requires, exhaustively: reader and writer surfaces; a cursor at the origin that stays at the origin; `next` returning values in order and never `gap`; `append!` returning `ok`. Nothing else. It needs no attachments (one process, handle passed directly), and its `ds/closed?` guards (`schema.cljc:318-326`, `:1069`, `:1127`) are the transactor plan's problem — v2 has no `closed?` predicate and this transport must not grow one.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Name, namespace, and the creation specification\n    25\t\n    26\t- Namespace: **`dao.stream.memory-log`** → `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc`\n    27\t- Transport type: **`:dao.stream/memory-log`**\n    28\t\n    29\tThe name states both halves of its nature, medium and retention, so it is mistaken for neither the ring buffer nor a durable log. v1's `dao.stream.file` was the durable one and this is not that; a later durable transport takes `dao.stream.file-log` / `:dao.stream/file-log`. Bare `:dao.stream/log` was rejected: it says complete retention and says nothing about surviving a restart, which is exactly the confusion §6 exists to prevent.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The specification rule.** The contract gives both halves of it: every non-`:dao.stream/type` key is \"transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace … and a handler that rejects its own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`\" (`dao.stream.md:248-251`), while envelopes are open and \"a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand\" (`:302-304`). So:\n    32\t\n    33\t- A key in **this transport's own namespace** — anything `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` — is **rejected** with `invalid-spec`. The transport owns that namespace and has nothing in it, so a key there is a caller believing in a knob that does not exist. The one they will reach for is a capacity, and that is precisely the mistake this transport exists to make impossible; failing loudly at creation beats ignoring it and appearing to honour it.\n    34\t- Any **other** qualified key is **ignored**, per the open-map rule.\n    35\t\n    36\t`valid-spec?` is therefore: a map, `:dao.stream/type` equal to `transport-type`, and no key namespaced `dao.stream.memory-log`.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 2. The manifest\n    39\t\n    40\tDeclared operations are `create!`, `descriptor`, `cursor`, `next`, `append!`, `close!`. **`attach!` is absent, not excluded** — the transport has no attach implementation, so there is no operation whose outcomes to declare. `validate-manifest` (`conformance.cljc:24`) requires only `:descriptor` plus surface-licensed operations, so an absent `:attach!` key is valid. A host dispatch table supplies no `:dao.stream/attach` for this type and `host-dispatch-attach!` answers `:dao.stream/not-found` — the contract's own answer for a descriptor with nothing behind it (`dao.stream.md:233-239`).\n    41\t\n    42\t```clojure\n    43\t(def memory-log-manifest\n    44\t  {:dao.stream/type log/transport-type\n    45\t   :surfaces #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    46\t   :retention :complete\n    47\t   :retention-values (vec (range 256))   ; values this transport accepts (§4)\n    48\t   :handle-factory handle\n    49\t   :operations\n    50\t   {:create!\n    51\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\n    52\t     :exclusions\n    53\t     {:dao.stream/not-found\n    54\t      \"host dispatch selects the transport; a call reaching this handler has already matched it\"\n    55\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    56\t      \"creation allocates one in-memory state value: it either completes and returns, or does not return\"}}\n    57\t\n    58\t    :descriptor {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}\n    59\t\n    60\t    :cursor\n    61\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-anchor}\n    62\t     :exclusions\n    63\t     {:dao.stream/closed\n    64\t      \"there are no attachments: every handle is the logical stream's owner, and an owner mints cursors after close so retained history stays readable\"\n    65\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    66\t      \"minting reads one in-memory state value and has no failure it could observe and return from\"}}\n    67\t\n    68\t    :next\n    69\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end\n    70\t                 :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor}\n    71\t     :exclusions\n    72\t     {:dao.stream/gap\n    73\t      \"retention is complete: no position is ever dropped, so no cursor can point at one that was\"\n    74\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    75\t      \"a read is one indexed lookup into retained state; there is no failure it could observe and return from\"}}\n    76\t\n    77\t    :append!\n    78\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/closed}\n    79\t     :exclusions\n    80\t     {:dao.stream/full\n    81\t      \"no capacity is declared: the transport is logically unbounded and refuses nothing\"\n    82\t      :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    83\t      \"the log holds host values by reference and encodes nothing, so no value is uncarriable\"\n    84\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    85\t      \"the append either completes its one state transition and returns, or does not return\"}}\n    86\t\n    87\t    :close! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}}\n    88\t\n    89\t   :fixtures\n    90\t   {:create! {:dao.stream/ok #(log/create! spec)\n    91\t              :dao.stream/invalid-spec\n    92\t              #(log/create! (assoc spec :dao.stream.memory-log/capacity 1024))}\n    93\t    :descriptor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/descriptor (handle))}\n    94\t    :cursor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/cursor (handle) :dao.stream/oldest)\n    95\t             :dao.stream/invalid-anchor #(stream/cursor (handle) ::invalid-anchor)}\n    96\t    :next {:dao.stream/ok #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n    97\t                             (stream/append! h :value)\n    98\t                             (stream/next h c))\n    99\t           :dao.stream/blocked #(let [h (handle)]\n   100\t                                  (stream/next h (cur h :dao.stream/newest)))\n   101\t           :dao.stream/end #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/newest)]\n   102\t                              (stream/close! h)\n   103\t                              (stream/next h c))\n   104\t           :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch #(let [l (handle) r (handle)]\n   105\t                                          (stream/next r (cur l :dao.stream/oldest)))\n   106\t           :dao.stream/invalid-cursor #(stream/next (handle) nil)}\n   107\t    :append! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/append! (handle) :value)\n   108\t              :dao.stream/closed #(let [h (handle)]\n   109\t                                    (stream/close! h)\n   110\t                                    (stream/append! h :value))}\n   111\t    :close! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/close! (handle))}}})\n   112\t```\n   113\t\n   114\tThe `invalid-spec` fixture is the own-namespace-key case because it encodes the §1 decision; the transport test covers all four rejection cases and the ignore case (§4).\n   115\t\n   116\t`gap`'s exclusion reason is the contract's first honest reason in substance: the outcome's precondition is impossible by the transport's nature (`dao.stream.md:442-445`). Every `transport-error` reason is phrased as the contract's own test (`:735-737`) rather than as a claim about the heap — see §3.\n   117\t\n   118\t## 3. Unbounded, `full`, and `transport-error`\n   119\t\n   120\t**Decision: logically unbounded; `full` and `transport-error` are both excluded.**\n   121\t\n   122\t### `transport-error`\n   123\t\n   124\tThe line is not between the transport's medium and the host's — the vector does live on that heap. The line the contract draws is between **a failure an operation can observe and return from** and **fatal host or runtime exhaustion from which the operation produces no result at all**; the first is inside the outcome algebra, the second outside it entirely (`dao.stream.md:729-737`, amended in the working tree).\n   125\t\n   126\tEvery operation of this transport sits on one side of that line. Each is a single state transition over in-memory values — one `swap!`, one deref, one indexed lookup — with no intermediate condition it could detect and report: it completes and returns a result, or it does not return. That is the contract's own stated test for excluding the outcome, and it holds identically on clj, cljs and cljd, which is what an exclusion must do.\n   127\t\n   128\tNor is exhaustion ever converted into eviction: there is no code path in this namespace that removes an element (§5), so the contract's \"never … the eviction of acknowledged history\" is structurally satisfied rather than promised.\n   129\t\n   130\t### `full`\n   131\t\n   132\tA finite complete-history log returning **permanent** `full` is legitimate — the contract says `full` may be transient or permanent, and the writer decides what to do with any non-`ok` outcome. (§4's law is now written to accept such a transport; r2's was not.)\n   133\t\n   134\tThe reason to exclude it here is a migration reason. v1's capacity-less local ring buffer never refused an append, so declaring a capacity would introduce a write failure `dao.space` has never seen and has no policy for: the transactor would have to abort a transaction or roll the log over, and neither policy exists. A migration that changes what `append!` can answer is not a migration. This transport declares no capacity, refuses nothing, and the contract permits the exclusion on exactly that basis (`dao.stream.md:727-729`).\n   135\t\n   136\t### What happens when the heap is exhausted, and what is not available\n   137\t\n   138\tNothing in this transport, and — stated plainly rather than delegated upward — **nothing in the composition either, today.** Rotation (\"publish a checkpoint and start a fresh stream\") is not available:\n   139\t\n   140\t- A new transactor over an empty log derives `t = 0` and collides with the prior history's transaction numbers.\n   141\t- Publishing only the new log omits everything before the rotation.\n   142\t- Queries would need a snapshot chain or a merge policy across rotations, and neither exists.\n   143\t\n   144\tRotation is future work requiring a checkpoint that carries causality forward plus an index and query composition designed around it. Until then, unbounded growth over a long-lived process is a **known, named limitation of this migration**, inherited unchanged from v1 — whose capacity-less ring buffer has exactly the same property — and not something this transport either introduces or can fix.\n   145\t\n   146\tNo transport-owned size or depth operation is added. The contract permits one (`dao.stream.md:400-405`) and it would be the first thing contract-generic code reached for.\n   147\t\n   148\t## 4. Conformance\n   149\t\n   150\t### What the existing suite covers, and what changes\n   151\t\n   152\tThe manifest passes `validate-manifest`: `produces ∪ exclusions` equals `v2/operation-outcomes` per declared op, the sets are disjoint, every exclusion has a non-empty reason.\n   153\t\n   154\tThree law blocks behave differently from the ring buffer's run:\n   155\t\n   156\t- `run-reader-laws` (`conformance.cljc:171`) mints `:oldest` on a fresh handle and reads it twice. Here `:oldest` is position 0 and stays there forever, so the block passes for a stronger reason than on the ring buffer, where the anchor tracks an advancing watermark.\n   157\t- `run-induction-coverage` (`:112`) needs no `gap` inducer, since `gap` is excluded.\n   158\t- `run-close-laws` (`:231`) closes the logical stream, not an attachment.\n   159\t\n   160\t**The abstract model needs no change.** `make-abstract-stream-model` (`:382`) already takes `capacity nil` and guards its trim with `(and (:capacity state) …)` (`:481-485`). With no attachments, `visible-tail` is `:next-pos` and `retained-first` is 0 once anything is appended (`:next-pos`, also 0, when empty), so the `:oldest` expectation `(min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))` (`:509`) is 0 at every state — the contract's \"a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin\", already encoded. Supply a `:cursor-projector` for this transport's cursor keys and `capacity nil`; nothing else.\n   161\t\n   162\tLinearizability histories, mirroring `bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories` (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:299`): concurrent append/append, append/next, append/close, and two cursors at position 0 read concurrently after one completed append. `run-concurrently` is private to `ringbuffer_test`; duplicate the twelve-line helper rather than promote it — consolidating the copies is a separate cleanup.\n   163\t\n   164\t### Why an addition is needed at all\n   165\t\n   166\t`run-induction-coverage` verifies that every **declared** outcome is inducible, and its \"no observed outcome falls outside `:produces`\" check only inspects results from fixtures the manifest itself supplies. **No fixture can be written for an outcome the manifest excludes**, so an exclusion is an unchecked assertion, and nothing in the harness can catch a transport that declares complete retention and evicts. (This principle is now recorded in `conformance.cljc`'s own namespace documentation — see *The exclusion principle* below — so it outlives this plan.)\n   167\t\n   168\t\"Never evicts\" is a negative over unboundedly many appends and is not testable in general. What is testable is the observation a kept origin cursor makes, which is the mechanism the whole *Complete history* section rests on.\n   169\t\n   170\t### `run-retention-laws`\n   171\t\n   172\tAdded to `conformance.cljc`, run from `run-conformance-suite`, and executed **only when the manifest carries a `:retention` key** — so every existing manifest is unaffected.\n   173\t\n   174\t**Declaration checks**, before any operation:\n   175\t\n   176\t- `:retention` must be a known value — currently `#{:complete}`. An unrecognized value is a violation (`:unknown-retention-declaration`), never a silent skip, so a typo cannot disable the laws.\n   177\t- `:complete` requires the `:reader` surface (`:complete-retention-without-reader`). Completeness is a promise to a cursor; a transport with no reader has no one to make it to.\n   178\t- `:complete` requires `:next` to exclude `gap` (`:complete-retention-declares-gap`).\n   179\t\n   180\t**Population — generalized (this revision).** The law needs a populated history and a cursor kept from before it existed. It must not assume the transport is unbounded, nor that it accepts arbitrary values.\n   181\t\n   182\t*Writer path* (`:writer` declared):\n   183\t\n   184\t1. `:retention-values` is **required** — a vector of values this transport accepts. It is not defaulted: a transport may restrict its value domain, and conformance tokens invented by the harness could legitimately draw `invalid-value`. Absent → `:missing-retention-values`.\n   185\t2. Mint `:dao.stream/oldest` on a fresh `:handle-factory` handle **first**. This is the pre-mutation origin cursor, and it is the whole point.\n   186\t3. Append the values in order, classifying each result:\n   187\t   - `ok` → collect the value into `expected`;\n   188\t   - `full` → **stop population and continue to the replay checks.** A declared-finite complete-history writer is conforming, and its refusal is not a retention failure. `expected` is what it accepted;\n   189\t   - `invalid-value` → skip that value and continue; completeness promises retention of *accepted* values, not acceptance of arbitrary ones;\n   190\t   - anything else (`closed`, `transport-error`) → `:unexpected-population-outcome`, stop.\n   191\t4. `expected` must be non-empty (`:no-accepted-values`), so the law cannot pass vacuously on a transport that refuses everything.\n   192\t5. Expected terminal outcome for an open stream is `:dao.stream/blocked` — including for a `full` finite log, where the tail is simply where its acceptance stopped.\n   193\t\n   194\t*Reader-only path* (`:writer` not declared — the contract explicitly permits a reader-only complete-history transport, \"a finite immutable history is a valid one\", `dao.stream.md:722-724`): the manifest must supply `:retention-fixture`, a thunk returning `{:handle h :expected [v …] :terminal :dao.stream/end}`. Its absence is a violation (`:missing-retention-fixture`), not a pass. `:terminal` is **required** and must be `:dao.stream/blocked` or `:dao.stream/end` (`:missing-retention-terminal`): a populated immutable reader may legitimately be either open or closed, and the law cannot guess which.\n   195\t\n   196\t**Replay checks.** A bounded helper follows a cursor to its terminus, collecting values and the set of outcomes seen, reporting `:did-not-terminate` past `(+ (count expected) 8)` iterations. Each check asserts three things, named separately:\n   197\t\n   198\t- no outcome in the replay was `gap` → `:gap-on-complete-retention`\n   199\t- the collected values equal `expected` → `:incomplete-replay`\n   200\t- the terminal outcome is the declared one → `:unexpected-terminal-outcome`\n   201\t\n   202\tTwo replays run: from the **kept origin cursor**, and from a **freshly minted `:oldest`**. The shared law never compares cursors structurally — representation is transport-owned and two cursors denoting one position need not be equal — it compares the two **observed sequences** against `expected`. Structural cursor equality is asserted only in the memory-log-specific test, where the representation is ours.\n   203\t\n   204\t**Close.** Where the law itself populated an owner handle and `:closable` is declared, it then closes and repeats both replays, now requiring terminal `end`. Completeness lasts the logical stream's lifetime, and `run-close-laws` only closes a fresh empty instance. If post-close `cursor` answers `closed` — a transport whose handles are attachments rather than owners — the fresh-`:oldest` half is skipped as inapplicable while the kept-origin half still runs, so nothing is waived. A **fixture-supplied** handle is never closed: the law does not own its lifecycle.\n   205\t\n   206\tSketch of the core, to pin the shape:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t(def ^:private retention-declarations #{:complete})\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn- replay-from\n   212\t  \"Follow a cursor to its terminus, bounded.  Observations only.\"\n   213\t  [handle cursor limit]\n   214\t  (loop [c cursor n 0 vs [] seen #{}]\n   215\t    (if (> n limit)\n   216\t      {:values vs :terminal ::did-not-terminate :outcomes seen}\n   217\t      (let [r (v2/next handle c)\n   218\t            o (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   219\t        (if (= o :dao.stream/ok)\n   220\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (inc n) (conj vs (:dao.stream/value r)) (conj seen o))\n   221\t          {:values vs :terminal o :outcomes (conj seen o)})))))\n   222\t\n   223\t(defn- populate\n   224\t  \"Append until refused.  Returns the values the transport accepted.\"\n   225\t  [handle values]\n   226\t  (reduce (fn [acc v]\n   227\t            (case (:dao.stream/outcome (v2/append! handle v))\n   228\t              :dao.stream/ok            (conj acc v)\n   229\t              :dao.stream/invalid-value acc\n   230\t              :dao.stream/full          (reduced acc)\n   231\t              (reduced (with-meta acc {::defect true}))))\n   232\t          [] values))\n   233\t```\n   234\t\n   235\tThe law now covers unbounded `memory-log`, finite complete writers that refuse, restricted value domains, and immutable readers, without over-certifying any of them.\n   236\t\n   237\t### The falsification test, committed\n   238\t\n   239\t`test/dao/stream/retention_falsification_test.cljc` — a dedicated namespace, because this tests the harness, not either transport.\n   240\t\n   241\tThe adversary must be a manifest that **lies**, not one that is merely inconsistent: if it declared `gap` in `:produces`, the declaration check would fire and the behavioural replay would never run. So the test builds, from the ring buffer's real manifest, a manifest with `:retention :complete`, `:retention-values (vec (range 256))`, and `:next` moved to exclude `gap` with the reason `\"false declaration under test\"`. Every declaration check then passes and only the replay can catch it.\n   242\t\n   243\tThe generalized population does not blunt this: a ring buffer **never returns `full`** — it evicts — so all 256 appends answer `ok`, `expected` is all 256 values, and the kept pre-eviction origin cursor's first `next` answers `gap` after capacity two. The test asserts `(false? (:passed? result))` and that some failure carries `:check :gap-on-complete-retention` — the specific P0 mechanism, observed through a cursor held from before the eviction, not merely \"something failed\". The unused `gap` fixture entry may stay: induction only looks up outcomes in `:produces`.\n   244\t\n   245\t### The exclusion principle, in `conformance.cljc`'s namespace documentation\n   246\t\n   247\tRecorded where it survives this plan's deletion, in prose rather than as new machinery:\n   248\t\n   249\t> Induction proves that every outcome a manifest **declares** is inducible. It cannot prove that an outcome a manifest **excludes** is impossible — an exclusion is discharged by the absence of a fixture, and absence proves nothing. Each exclusion is therefore a proof obligation, discharged by one of three things: a structural argument about the transport (there is no code path that could produce it), a property law such as complete retention that would observe the outcome if it fired, or a transport-specific falsification test. Fixtures must never be required for excluded outcomes: a fixture inducing one would contradict the exclusion it is meant to support.\n   250\t\n   251\t### The transport's own tests (`memory_log_test.cljc`)\n   252\t\n   253\t- `complete-retention`: origin cursor, 1000 appends, full replay with no `gap`; a freshly minted `:oldest` is **structurally equal** to the origin cursor (permitted here, where the representation is ours); the same after `close!`, terminating in `end`.\n   254\t- `next-is-total`: fabricated cursors with the right identity and position `-1` → `invalid-cursor`; `(inc tail)` → `invalid-cursor`; `tail` → `blocked` open, `end` closed; non-integer → `invalid-cursor`; `nil` → `invalid-cursor`; foreign identity → `cursor-mismatch`. A `.cljc` test with no host conditional, so all three hosts run it.\n   255\t- `creation-spec-rule`: non-map, missing `:dao.stream/type`, wrong type, and a `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` key each → `invalid-spec`; a foreign qualified key (`:my.host/label`) → `ok`.\n   256\t- Conformance suite and linearizability histories as above.\n   257\t\n   258\t## 5. Cursors and reading\n   259\t\n   260\tState is the minimum that can express this transport:\n   261\t\n   262\t```clojure\n   263\t{:identity (str (random-uuid))   ; portable data, as in ringbuffer.cljc:27\n   264\t :values   []                    ; dense from position 0\n   265\t :closed?  false}\n   266\t```\n   267\t\n   268\tA **vector**, not the ring buffer's map: positions are dense and nothing is ever removed, so `conj` and `nth` suffice and the tail is `(count values)`. There is no `:first` watermark because there is nothing to watermark, and no `:attachments` because there are none. The absence of eviction is structural — the namespace contains no expression that removes an element — rather than a policy chosen at each append.\n   269\t\n   270\t- **Positions** are integers from 0, dense, absolute, never reused, never dropped.\n   271\t- **Cursor value**: `{:dao.stream.memory-log/identity <id> :dao.stream.memory-log/position <n>}`. The identity is the logical-stream identity — the same value `descriptor` projects under `:dao.stream/identity` and the same one `cursor-mismatch` compares — under a transport-owned key, because cursor representation belongs to the transport.\n   272\t- **`:oldest`** is position 0, always, for the life of the stream, before the first append and after close. A fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin, which is what `dao.space`'s from-zero rescans need.\n   273\t- **`:newest`** is `(count values)`.\n   274\t- **Attach and frozen tails: none.** Every handle is the logical stream's owner, so `cursor` never answers `:dao.stream/closed` and no handle has a frozen visible tail.\n   275\t- **After close**, `cursor` keeps minting; `:oldest` is still 0, `:newest` still the tail, and a `next` at the tail answers `end` instead of `blocked`.\n   276\t\n   277\t### `next` is total\n   278\t\n   279\tr1's validation accepted every integer position and reached `nth` with it — a cursor carrying the right identity and position `-1` would have thrown on the JVM and behaved differently on each host. The corrected order, with the range checked **before** any indexed read:\n   280\t\n   281\t1. not a map → `invalid-cursor`\n   282\t2. no `:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` key → `invalid-cursor`\n   283\t3. identity ≠ this stream's → `cursor-mismatch`\n   284\t4. position not an integer, or `< 0`, or `> tail` → `invalid-cursor`\n   285\t5. `0 <= pos < tail` → `ok` with the value and the successor cursor\n   286\t6. `pos = tail` → `end` if closed, else `blocked`\n   287\t\n   288\tStep 4's upper bound is not merely defensive. On this transport positions never disappear, the tail never retreats, and there are no frozen attachment tails, so **no cursor this logical stream can mint holds a position above its current tail**; such a value is a fabrication, and the contract's cursor-provenance rule says consumers never fabricate. The ring buffer's more permissive `blocked` at the same input creates no cross-transport law — cursor validity and representation are transport-owned, the two transports' cursor keys differ, and conforming consumers construct neither.\n   289\t\n   290\t**Caveat for whoever extends the oracle.** The shared abstract model classifies every `pos >= tail` as `blocked`/`end` (`conformance.cljc:558-561`). None of the linearizability histories in this plan supplies a fabricated future cursor, so nothing relies on that classification today. If invalid-cursor histories are ever added, the model will need a per-transport option or a stricter range rule — recorded here rather than pre-built, because building it now would be machinery for a history no one has written.\n   291\t\n   292\t**`append!`** takes the ring buffer's single-linearization-point shape (`ringbuffer.cljc:117-132`): one `swap!` whose function checks `:closed?` and either records `closed` or `conj`s the value, with the outcome carried out in a `volatile!`. A deref before the swap would let an append that began before a close land after it.\n   293\t\n   294\t### Host portability\n   295\t\n   296\tThe transport source needs **no reader conditionals at all** — `deftype`, protocol implementation, `atom`/`swap!`, `volatile!`, `random-uuid`, `conj`, `nth`, `count` are all used unguarded by `ringbuffer.cljc` across clj, cljs and cljd today. That is the safest position with respect to the `#?(:clj …)` trap, since the trap only bites where a conditional is added. The one `#?` in this work is the JVM-thread block in the test, written `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` **first**, as `ringbuffer_test.cljc:252,277,297` already does.\n   297\t\n   298\t## 6. What it does not do\n   299\t\n   300\t- **No durability across process restart.** The log is one atom; the process dying takes it. A restarted host sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n   301\t- **No eviction, no `gap`, no `full`, no capacity key** — and a `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` key in a creation spec is rejected rather than ignored, so a caller cannot quietly ask for one.\n   302\t- **No attachments**: no `attach!`, no attachment identities, no frozen tails, no `:dao.stream/closed` from `cursor`.\n   303\t- **No encoding.** Values are held by reference, so this is not a serialization boundary; a live handle can pass through it, and no value is uncarriable.\n   304\t- **No size, depth, or retention query**, and no `closed?` predicate.\n   305\t- **No readiness or waiter mechanism** — the contract has none.\n   306\t- **No rotation, and no support for it** (§3).\n   307\t\n   308\tA later **durable** transport is a different transport, not this one grown. It would add: a medium that survives restart, and therefore an identity recovered from the medium rather than minted at creation; `attach!` resolving against the filesystem rather than a host directory; `:dao.stream/transport-error` on `cursor`, `next` and `append!` — a durable read is exactly a failure an operation can observe and return from, and locating the earliest retained position on a durable log is itself such a read (`dao.stream.md:483-488`); `:dao.stream/invalid-value`, because it encodes and is therefore a serialization boundary; a real `:dao.stream/full`, because a disk is genuinely bounded; and a `close!` that flushes, hence a named channel where flush failure surfaces (`dao.stream.md:583-586`). Nothing in `dao.stream.memory-log` should be shaped to anticipate any of that.\n   309\t\n   310\t## 7. Phasing and the handoff\n   311\t\n   312\t**Phase A, two commits.**\n   313\t\n   314\t1. `docs(stream): distinguish observable exhaustion from fatal host failure` — the *Complete history* amendment already sitting uncommitted in the working tree (`dao.stream.md:729-737`).\n   315\t2. `add the memory-log complete-retention transport` — `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/memory_log_test.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/retention_falsification_test.cljc`, and, in `test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc`, `run-retention-laws` plus the exclusion principle in the namespace documentation. `dao.stream.ringbuffer` is untouched.\n   316\t\n   317\tNo new design doc: the namespace docstring and the manifest are this transport's specification, and `dao.stream.file.md` documents a far larger thing.\n   318\t\n   319\t**Phase B.** The transactor plan is revised against it. The sentence it can quote:\n   320\t\n   321\t> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n   322\t\n   323\tOwnership is preserved deliberately: `dao.space` does not create its local stream and must not start. What that plan must **not** ask of the transport: durability across restart, a size or retention query, a `closed?` predicate, a capacity, or rotation — which §3 records as unavailable, not as delegated.\n   324\t\n   325\t## Verification\n   326\t\n   327\t- `bb test:clj` — JVM: conformance suite including the new retention laws, the falsification test, the totality tests, the creation-spec rule, and the concurrent linearizability histories.\n   328\t- `bb test:cljs` — Node; confirm `Testing dao.stream.memory-log-test` and `Testing dao.stream.retention-falsification-test` both appear in the output, since the shadow `:node-test` build discovers namespaces rather than reading an allowlist.\n   329\t- `bb test:cljd` — Dart; the same, minus the thread-based block. The negative and beyond-tail position tests carry no host conditional and must run on all three.\n   330\t- Regression: `dao.stream.ringbuffer-test` must pass unchanged — `run-retention-laws` runs only for a manifest carrying `:retention`, and the real ring manifest carries none.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m applying the repository’s code-review checklist to the revised law, focusing on whether each admitted transport shape is genuinely exercised rather than passing vacuously. I’ll keep this read-only and won’t rerun tests."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && nl -ba collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,360p' && git diff -- docs/design/dao.stream.md && git status --short && date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT' && TZ=Asia/Bangkok date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z (%Z)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && nl -ba collab/1788878712118-architect-v2-memory-log-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,360p' && git diff -- docs/design/dao.stream.md && git status --short && date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT' && TZ=Asia/Bangkok date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z (%Z)'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n     1\t---\n     2\t\n     3\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-08 15:04 GMT\n     4\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-08 22:04 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     5\tCoding-Agent: claude (Opus 5)\n     6\tSession-ID: 0dae45ea-4204-49d9-bf35-c56e24cce14e\n     7\t\n     8\t# A complete-retention DaoStream v2 transport (r3)\n     9\t\n    10\tr2's six findings are confirmed closed. This revision changes `run-retention-laws` only, and folds in two rulings as prose. The correction is the same species as the P0 that started this work: a law generalized from one instance, quietly encoding that instance's properties. r2's law appended 256 values and compared the replay against all 256 — which would have failed a conforming capacity-2 complete-history writer after its second append, exactly the transport §3 argues is legitimate. Everything else stands.\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. The scoping judgement: confirmed\n    13\t\n    14\tVerified against the tree, not taken on report.\n    15\t\n    16\t- v1 local streams are opened bare: `(ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer})` with no `:capacity` — `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:219` and fifteen further sites, `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:569`, `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:103`. `dao.space` itself never creates one; the transactor takes it as `:local-stream`, \"supplied, never created, registered, or closed\" (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:8-14`).\n    17\t- v1 eviction is guarded by `(and capacity …)` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:76-91`), so a nil capacity short-circuits the branch: `:head` never advances, nothing is evicted, `:full` is unreachable and `:daostream/gap` is unreachable.\n    18\t- Nothing persists that stream. `publish-index!` (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:540-604`) snapshots it from position 0 (`snapshot-datoms`, `:442-468`) and emits b-tree blobs to a **v2** DaoJing intake stream; durability is entirely on the jing side. The code already says why complete retention is load-bearing (`index.cljc:561-564`). `derive-next-t` (`transactor.cljc:125-139`) and `schema/proposed-schema` (`schema.cljc:926`) rescan from zero for the same reason.\n    19\t\n    20\tSo the local stream is in-memory, unbounded, append-only, and dies with the process; a restarted process today derives `next-t` = 0 from an empty stream. **The faithful v2 counterpart is an in-memory, unbounded, append-only log.** A file-backed durable log would add semantics `dao.space` does not have and has not specified — it would silently change what a restart means — and durability across restarts is `dao.jing`'s job, which `dao.space` already composes with for publication.\n    21\t\n    22\tWhat `dao.space` actually requires, exhaustively: reader and writer surfaces; a cursor at the origin that stays at the origin; `next` returning values in order and never `gap`; `append!` returning `ok`. Nothing else. It needs no attachments (one process, handle passed directly), and its `ds/closed?` guards (`schema.cljc:318-326`, `:1069`, `:1127`) are the transactor plan's problem — v2 has no `closed?` predicate and this transport must not grow one.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Name, namespace, and the creation specification\n    25\t\n    26\t- Namespace: **`dao.stream.memory-log`** → `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc`\n    27\t- Transport type: **`:dao.stream/memory-log`**\n    28\t\n    29\tThe name states both halves of its nature, medium and retention, so it is mistaken for neither the ring buffer nor a durable log. v1's `dao.stream.file` was the durable one and this is not that; a later durable transport takes `dao.stream.file-log` / `:dao.stream/file-log`. Bare `:dao.stream/log` was rejected: it says complete retention and says nothing about surviving a restart, which is exactly the confusion §6 exists to prevent.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The specification rule.** The contract gives both halves of it: every non-`:dao.stream/type` key is \"transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace … and a handler that rejects its own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`\" (`dao.stream.md:248-251`), while envelopes are open and \"a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand\" (`:302-304`). So:\n    32\t\n    33\t- A key in **this transport's own namespace** — anything `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` — is **rejected** with `invalid-spec`. The transport owns that namespace and has nothing in it, so a key there is a caller believing in a knob that does not exist. The one they will reach for is a capacity, and that is precisely the mistake this transport exists to make impossible; failing loudly at creation beats ignoring it and appearing to honour it.\n    34\t- Any **other** qualified key is **ignored**, per the open-map rule.\n    35\t\n    36\t`valid-spec?` is therefore: a map, `:dao.stream/type` equal to `transport-type`, and no key namespaced `dao.stream.memory-log`.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 2. The manifest\n    39\t\n    40\tDeclared operations are `create!`, `descriptor`, `cursor`, `next`, `append!`, `close!`. **`attach!` is absent, not excluded** — the transport has no attach implementation, so there is no operation whose outcomes to declare. `validate-manifest` (`conformance.cljc:24`) requires only `:descriptor` plus surface-licensed operations, so an absent `:attach!` key is valid. A host dispatch table supplies no `:dao.stream/attach` for this type and `host-dispatch-attach!` answers `:dao.stream/not-found` — the contract's own answer for a descriptor with nothing behind it (`dao.stream.md:233-239`).\n    41\t\n    42\t```clojure\n    43\t(def memory-log-manifest\n    44\t  {:dao.stream/type log/transport-type\n    45\t   :surfaces #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    46\t   :retention :complete\n    47\t   :retention-values (vec (range 256))   ; values this transport accepts (§4)\n    48\t   :handle-factory handle\n    49\t   :operations\n    50\t   {:create!\n    51\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\n    52\t     :exclusions\n    53\t     {:dao.stream/not-found\n    54\t      \"host dispatch selects the transport; a call reaching this handler has already matched it\"\n    55\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    56\t      \"creation allocates one in-memory state value: it either completes and returns, or does not return\"}}\n    57\t\n    58\t    :descriptor {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}\n    59\t\n    60\t    :cursor\n    61\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-anchor}\n    62\t     :exclusions\n    63\t     {:dao.stream/closed\n    64\t      \"there are no attachments: every handle is the logical stream's owner, and an owner mints cursors after close so retained history stays readable\"\n    65\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    66\t      \"minting reads one in-memory state value and has no failure it could observe and return from\"}}\n    67\t\n    68\t    :next\n    69\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end\n    70\t                 :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor}\n    71\t     :exclusions\n    72\t     {:dao.stream/gap\n    73\t      \"retention is complete: no position is ever dropped, so no cursor can point at one that was\"\n    74\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    75\t      \"a read is one indexed lookup into retained state; there is no failure it could observe and return from\"}}\n    76\t\n    77\t    :append!\n    78\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/closed}\n    79\t     :exclusions\n    80\t     {:dao.stream/full\n    81\t      \"no capacity is declared: the transport is logically unbounded and refuses nothing\"\n    82\t      :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    83\t      \"the log holds host values by reference and encodes nothing, so no value is uncarriable\"\n    84\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    85\t      \"the append either completes its one state transition and returns, or does not return\"}}\n    86\t\n    87\t    :close! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}}\n    88\t\n    89\t   :fixtures\n    90\t   {:create! {:dao.stream/ok #(log/create! spec)\n    91\t              :dao.stream/invalid-spec\n    92\t              #(log/create! (assoc spec :dao.stream.memory-log/capacity 1024))}\n    93\t    :descriptor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/descriptor (handle))}\n    94\t    :cursor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/cursor (handle) :dao.stream/oldest)\n    95\t             :dao.stream/invalid-anchor #(stream/cursor (handle) ::invalid-anchor)}\n    96\t    :next {:dao.stream/ok #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n    97\t                             (stream/append! h :value)\n    98\t                             (stream/next h c))\n    99\t           :dao.stream/blocked #(let [h (handle)]\n   100\t                                  (stream/next h (cur h :dao.stream/newest)))\n   101\t           :dao.stream/end #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/newest)]\n   102\t                              (stream/close! h)\n   103\t                              (stream/next h c))\n   104\t           :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch #(let [l (handle) r (handle)]\n   105\t                                          (stream/next r (cur l :dao.stream/oldest)))\n   106\t           :dao.stream/invalid-cursor #(stream/next (handle) nil)}\n   107\t    :append! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/append! (handle) :value)\n   108\t              :dao.stream/closed #(let [h (handle)]\n   109\t                                    (stream/close! h)\n   110\t                                    (stream/append! h :value))}\n   111\t    :close! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/close! (handle))}}})\n   112\t```\n   113\t\n   114\tThe `invalid-spec` fixture is the own-namespace-key case because it encodes the §1 decision; the transport test covers all four rejection cases and the ignore case (§4).\n   115\t\n   116\t`gap`'s exclusion reason is the contract's first honest reason in substance: the outcome's precondition is impossible by the transport's nature (`dao.stream.md:442-445`). Every `transport-error` reason is phrased as the contract's own test (`:735-737`) rather than as a claim about the heap — see §3.\n   117\t\n   118\t## 3. Unbounded, `full`, and `transport-error`\n   119\t\n   120\t**Decision: logically unbounded; `full` and `transport-error` are both excluded.**\n   121\t\n   122\t### `transport-error`\n   123\t\n   124\tThe line is not between the transport's medium and the host's — the vector does live on that heap. The line the contract draws is between **a failure an operation can observe and return from** and **fatal host or runtime exhaustion from which the operation produces no result at all**; the first is inside the outcome algebra, the second outside it entirely (`dao.stream.md:729-737`, amended in the working tree).\n   125\t\n   126\tEvery operation of this transport sits on one side of that line. Each is a single state transition over in-memory values — one `swap!`, one deref, one indexed lookup — with no intermediate condition it could detect and report: it completes and returns a result, or it does not return. That is the contract's own stated test for excluding the outcome, and it holds identically on clj, cljs and cljd, which is what an exclusion must do.\n   127\t\n   128\tNor is exhaustion ever converted into eviction: there is no code path in this namespace that removes an element (§5), so the contract's \"never … the eviction of acknowledged history\" is structurally satisfied rather than promised.\n   129\t\n   130\t### `full`\n   131\t\n   132\tA finite complete-history log returning **permanent** `full` is legitimate — the contract says `full` may be transient or permanent, and the writer decides what to do with any non-`ok` outcome. (§4's law is now written to accept such a transport; r2's was not.)\n   133\t\n   134\tThe reason to exclude it here is a migration reason. v1's capacity-less local ring buffer never refused an append, so declaring a capacity would introduce a write failure `dao.space` has never seen and has no policy for: the transactor would have to abort a transaction or roll the log over, and neither policy exists. A migration that changes what `append!` can answer is not a migration. This transport declares no capacity, refuses nothing, and the contract permits the exclusion on exactly that basis (`dao.stream.md:727-729`).\n   135\t\n   136\t### What happens when the heap is exhausted, and what is not available\n   137\t\n   138\tNothing in this transport, and — stated plainly rather than delegated upward — **nothing in the composition either, today.** Rotation (\"publish a checkpoint and start a fresh stream\") is not available:\n   139\t\n   140\t- A new transactor over an empty log derives `t = 0` and collides with the prior history's transaction numbers.\n   141\t- Publishing only the new log omits everything before the rotation.\n   142\t- Queries would need a snapshot chain or a merge policy across rotations, and neither exists.\n   143\t\n   144\tRotation is future work requiring a checkpoint that carries causality forward plus an index and query composition designed around it. Until then, unbounded growth over a long-lived process is a **known, named limitation of this migration**, inherited unchanged from v1 — whose capacity-less ring buffer has exactly the same property — and not something this transport either introduces or can fix.\n   145\t\n   146\tNo transport-owned size or depth operation is added. The contract permits one (`dao.stream.md:400-405`) and it would be the first thing contract-generic code reached for.\n   147\t\n   148\t## 4. Conformance\n   149\t\n   150\t### What the existing suite covers, and what changes\n   151\t\n   152\tThe manifest passes `validate-manifest`: `produces ∪ exclusions` equals `v2/operation-outcomes` per declared op, the sets are disjoint, every exclusion has a non-empty reason.\n   153\t\n   154\tThree law blocks behave differently from the ring buffer's run:\n   155\t\n   156\t- `run-reader-laws` (`conformance.cljc:171`) mints `:oldest` on a fresh handle and reads it twice. Here `:oldest` is position 0 and stays there forever, so the block passes for a stronger reason than on the ring buffer, where the anchor tracks an advancing watermark.\n   157\t- `run-induction-coverage` (`:112`) needs no `gap` inducer, since `gap` is excluded.\n   158\t- `run-close-laws` (`:231`) closes the logical stream, not an attachment.\n   159\t\n   160\t**The abstract model needs no change.** `make-abstract-stream-model` (`:382`) already takes `capacity nil` and guards its trim with `(and (:capacity state) …)` (`:481-485`). With no attachments, `visible-tail` is `:next-pos` and `retained-first` is 0 once anything is appended (`:next-pos`, also 0, when empty), so the `:oldest` expectation `(min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))` (`:509`) is 0 at every state — the contract's \"a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin\", already encoded. Supply a `:cursor-projector` for this transport's cursor keys and `capacity nil`; nothing else.\n   161\t\n   162\tLinearizability histories, mirroring `bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories` (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:299`): concurrent append/append, append/next, append/close, and two cursors at position 0 read concurrently after one completed append. `run-concurrently` is private to `ringbuffer_test`; duplicate the twelve-line helper rather than promote it — consolidating the copies is a separate cleanup.\n   163\t\n   164\t### Why an addition is needed at all\n   165\t\n   166\t`run-induction-coverage` verifies that every **declared** outcome is inducible, and its \"no observed outcome falls outside `:produces`\" check only inspects results from fixtures the manifest itself supplies. **No fixture can be written for an outcome the manifest excludes**, so an exclusion is an unchecked assertion, and nothing in the harness can catch a transport that declares complete retention and evicts. (This principle is now recorded in `conformance.cljc`'s own namespace documentation — see *The exclusion principle* below — so it outlives this plan.)\n   167\t\n   168\t\"Never evicts\" is a negative over unboundedly many appends and is not testable in general. What is testable is the observation a kept origin cursor makes, which is the mechanism the whole *Complete history* section rests on.\n   169\t\n   170\t### `run-retention-laws`\n   171\t\n   172\tAdded to `conformance.cljc`, run from `run-conformance-suite`, and executed **only when the manifest carries a `:retention` key** — so every existing manifest is unaffected.\n   173\t\n   174\t**Declaration checks**, before any operation:\n   175\t\n   176\t- `:retention` must be a known value — currently `#{:complete}`. An unrecognized value is a violation (`:unknown-retention-declaration`), never a silent skip, so a typo cannot disable the laws.\n   177\t- `:complete` requires the `:reader` surface (`:complete-retention-without-reader`). Completeness is a promise to a cursor; a transport with no reader has no one to make it to.\n   178\t- `:complete` requires `:next` to exclude `gap` (`:complete-retention-declares-gap`).\n   179\t\n   180\t**Population — generalized (this revision).** The law needs a populated history and a cursor kept from before it existed. It must not assume the transport is unbounded, nor that it accepts arbitrary values.\n   181\t\n   182\t*Writer path* (`:writer` declared):\n   183\t\n   184\t1. `:retention-values` is **required** — a vector of values this transport accepts. It is not defaulted: a transport may restrict its value domain, and conformance tokens invented by the harness could legitimately draw `invalid-value`. Absent → `:missing-retention-values`.\n   185\t2. Mint `:dao.stream/oldest` on a fresh `:handle-factory` handle **first**. This is the pre-mutation origin cursor, and it is the whole point.\n   186\t3. Append the values in order, classifying each result:\n   187\t   - `ok` → collect the value into `expected`;\n   188\t   - `full` → **stop population and continue to the replay checks.** A declared-finite complete-history writer is conforming, and its refusal is not a retention failure. `expected` is what it accepted;\n   189\t   - `invalid-value` → skip that value and continue; completeness promises retention of *accepted* values, not acceptance of arbitrary ones;\n   190\t   - anything else (`closed`, `transport-error`) → `:unexpected-population-outcome`, stop.\n   191\t4. `expected` must be non-empty (`:no-accepted-values`), so the law cannot pass vacuously on a transport that refuses everything.\n   192\t5. Expected terminal outcome for an open stream is `:dao.stream/blocked` — including for a `full` finite log, where the tail is simply where its acceptance stopped.\n   193\t\n   194\t*Reader-only path* (`:writer` not declared — the contract explicitly permits a reader-only complete-history transport, \"a finite immutable history is a valid one\", `dao.stream.md:722-724`): the manifest must supply `:retention-fixture`, a thunk returning `{:handle h :expected [v …] :terminal :dao.stream/end}`. Its absence is a violation (`:missing-retention-fixture`), not a pass. `:terminal` is **required** and must be `:dao.stream/blocked` or `:dao.stream/end` (`:missing-retention-terminal`): a populated immutable reader may legitimately be either open or closed, and the law cannot guess which.\n   195\t\n   196\t**Replay checks.** A bounded helper follows a cursor to its terminus, collecting values and the set of outcomes seen, reporting `:did-not-terminate` past `(+ (count expected) 8)` iterations. Each check asserts three things, named separately:\n   197\t\n   198\t- no outcome in the replay was `gap` → `:gap-on-complete-retention`\n   199\t- the collected values equal `expected` → `:incomplete-replay`\n   200\t- the terminal outcome is the declared one → `:unexpected-terminal-outcome`\n   201\t\n   202\tTwo replays run: from the **kept origin cursor**, and from a **freshly minted `:oldest`**. The shared law never compares cursors structurally — representation is transport-owned and two cursors denoting one position need not be equal — it compares the two **observed sequences** against `expected`. Structural cursor equality is asserted only in the memory-log-specific test, where the representation is ours.\n   203\t\n   204\t**Close.** Where the law itself populated an owner handle and `:closable` is declared, it then closes and repeats both replays, now requiring terminal `end`. Completeness lasts the logical stream's lifetime, and `run-close-laws` only closes a fresh empty instance. If post-close `cursor` answers `closed` — a transport whose handles are attachments rather than owners — the fresh-`:oldest` half is skipped as inapplicable while the kept-origin half still runs, so nothing is waived. A **fixture-supplied** handle is never closed: the law does not own its lifecycle.\n   205\t\n   206\tSketch of the core, to pin the shape:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t(def ^:private retention-declarations #{:complete})\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn- replay-from\n   212\t  \"Follow a cursor to its terminus, bounded.  Observations only.\"\n   213\t  [handle cursor limit]\n   214\t  (loop [c cursor n 0 vs [] seen #{}]\n   215\t    (if (> n limit)\n   216\t      {:values vs :terminal ::did-not-terminate :outcomes seen}\n   217\t      (let [r (v2/next handle c)\n   218\t            o (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   219\t        (if (= o :dao.stream/ok)\n   220\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (inc n) (conj vs (:dao.stream/value r)) (conj seen o))\n   221\t          {:values vs :terminal o :outcomes (conj seen o)})))))\n   222\t\n   223\t(defn- populate\n   224\t  \"Append until refused.  Returns the values the transport accepted.\"\n   225\t  [handle values]\n   226\t  (reduce (fn [acc v]\n   227\t            (case (:dao.stream/outcome (v2/append! handle v))\n   228\t              :dao.stream/ok            (conj acc v)\n   229\t              :dao.stream/invalid-value acc\n   230\t              :dao.stream/full          (reduced acc)\n   231\t              (reduced (with-meta acc {::defect true}))))\n   232\t          [] values))\n   233\t```\n   234\t\n   235\tThe law now covers unbounded `memory-log`, finite complete writers that refuse, restricted value domains, and immutable readers, without over-certifying any of them.\n   236\t\n   237\t### The falsification test, committed\n   238\t\n   239\t`test/dao/stream/retention_falsification_test.cljc` — a dedicated namespace, because this tests the harness, not either transport.\n   240\t\n   241\tThe adversary must be a manifest that **lies**, not one that is merely inconsistent: if it declared `gap` in `:produces`, the declaration check would fire and the behavioural replay would never run. So the test builds, from the ring buffer's real manifest, a manifest with `:retention :complete`, `:retention-values (vec (range 256))`, and `:next` moved to exclude `gap` with the reason `\"false declaration under test\"`. Every declaration check then passes and only the replay can catch it.\n   242\t\n   243\tThe generalized population does not blunt this: a ring buffer **never returns `full`** — it evicts — so all 256 appends answer `ok`, `expected` is all 256 values, and the kept pre-eviction origin cursor's first `next` answers `gap` after capacity two. The test asserts `(false? (:passed? result))` and that some failure carries `:check :gap-on-complete-retention` — the specific P0 mechanism, observed through a cursor held from before the eviction, not merely \"something failed\". The unused `gap` fixture entry may stay: induction only looks up outcomes in `:produces`.\n   244\t\n   245\t### The exclusion principle, in `conformance.cljc`'s namespace documentation\n   246\t\n   247\tRecorded where it survives this plan's deletion, in prose rather than as new machinery:\n   248\t\n   249\t> Induction proves that every outcome a manifest **declares** is inducible. It cannot prove that an outcome a manifest **excludes** is impossible — an exclusion is discharged by the absence of a fixture, and absence proves nothing. Each exclusion is therefore a proof obligation, discharged by one of three things: a structural argument about the transport (there is no code path that could produce it), a property law such as complete retention that would observe the outcome if it fired, or a transport-specific falsification test. Fixtures must never be required for excluded outcomes: a fixture inducing one would contradict the exclusion it is meant to support.\n   250\t\n   251\t### The transport's own tests (`memory_log_test.cljc`)\n   252\t\n   253\t- `complete-retention`: origin cursor, 1000 appends, full replay with no `gap`; a freshly minted `:oldest` is **structurally equal** to the origin cursor (permitted here, where the representation is ours); the same after `close!`, terminating in `end`.\n   254\t- `next-is-total`: fabricated cursors with the right identity and position `-1` → `invalid-cursor`; `(inc tail)` → `invalid-cursor`; `tail` → `blocked` open, `end` closed; non-integer → `invalid-cursor`; `nil` → `invalid-cursor`; foreign identity → `cursor-mismatch`. A `.cljc` test with no host conditional, so all three hosts run it.\n   255\t- `creation-spec-rule`: non-map, missing `:dao.stream/type`, wrong type, and a `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` key each → `invalid-spec`; a foreign qualified key (`:my.host/label`) → `ok`.\n   256\t- Conformance suite and linearizability histories as above.\n   257\t\n   258\t## 5. Cursors and reading\n   259\t\n   260\tState is the minimum that can express this transport:\n   261\t\n   262\t```clojure\n   263\t{:identity (str (random-uuid))   ; portable data, as in ringbuffer.cljc:27\n   264\t :values   []                    ; dense from position 0\n   265\t :closed?  false}\n   266\t```\n   267\t\n   268\tA **vector**, not the ring buffer's map: positions are dense and nothing is ever removed, so `conj` and `nth` suffice and the tail is `(count values)`. There is no `:first` watermark because there is nothing to watermark, and no `:attachments` because there are none. The absence of eviction is structural — the namespace contains no expression that removes an element — rather than a policy chosen at each append.\n   269\t\n   270\t- **Positions** are integers from 0, dense, absolute, never reused, never dropped.\n   271\t- **Cursor value**: `{:dao.stream.memory-log/identity <id> :dao.stream.memory-log/position <n>}`. The identity is the logical-stream identity — the same value `descriptor` projects under `:dao.stream/identity` and the same one `cursor-mismatch` compares — under a transport-owned key, because cursor representation belongs to the transport.\n   272\t- **`:oldest`** is position 0, always, for the life of the stream, before the first append and after close. A fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin, which is what `dao.space`'s from-zero rescans need.\n   273\t- **`:newest`** is `(count values)`.\n   274\t- **Attach and frozen tails: none.** Every handle is the logical stream's owner, so `cursor` never answers `:dao.stream/closed` and no handle has a frozen visible tail.\n   275\t- **After close**, `cursor` keeps minting; `:oldest` is still 0, `:newest` still the tail, and a `next` at the tail answers `end` instead of `blocked`.\n   276\t\n   277\t### `next` is total\n   278\t\n   279\tr1's validation accepted every integer position and reached `nth` with it — a cursor carrying the right identity and position `-1` would have thrown on the JVM and behaved differently on each host. The corrected order, with the range checked **before** any indexed read:\n   280\t\n   281\t1. not a map → `invalid-cursor`\n   282\t2. no `:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` key → `invalid-cursor`\n   283\t3. identity ≠ this stream's → `cursor-mismatch`\n   284\t4. position not an integer, or `< 0`, or `> tail` → `invalid-cursor`\n   285\t5. `0 <= pos < tail` → `ok` with the value and the successor cursor\n   286\t6. `pos = tail` → `end` if closed, else `blocked`\n   287\t\n   288\tStep 4's upper bound is not merely defensive. On this transport positions never disappear, the tail never retreats, and there are no frozen attachment tails, so **no cursor this logical stream can mint holds a position above its current tail**; such a value is a fabrication, and the contract's cursor-provenance rule says consumers never fabricate. The ring buffer's more permissive `blocked` at the same input creates no cross-transport law — cursor validity and representation are transport-owned, the two transports' cursor keys differ, and conforming consumers construct neither.\n   289\t\n   290\t**Caveat for whoever extends the oracle.** The shared abstract model classifies every `pos >= tail` as `blocked`/`end` (`conformance.cljc:558-561`). None of the linearizability histories in this plan supplies a fabricated future cursor, so nothing relies on that classification today. If invalid-cursor histories are ever added, the model will need a per-transport option or a stricter range rule — recorded here rather than pre-built, because building it now would be machinery for a history no one has written.\n   291\t\n   292\t**`append!`** takes the ring buffer's single-linearization-point shape (`ringbuffer.cljc:117-132`): one `swap!` whose function checks `:closed?` and either records `closed` or `conj`s the value, with the outcome carried out in a `volatile!`. A deref before the swap would let an append that began before a close land after it.\n   293\t\n   294\t### Host portability\n   295\t\n   296\tThe transport source needs **no reader conditionals at all** — `deftype`, protocol implementation, `atom`/`swap!`, `volatile!`, `random-uuid`, `conj`, `nth`, `count` are all used unguarded by `ringbuffer.cljc` across clj, cljs and cljd today. That is the safest position with respect to the `#?(:clj …)` trap, since the trap only bites where a conditional is added. The one `#?` in this work is the JVM-thread block in the test, written `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` **first**, as `ringbuffer_test.cljc:252,277,297` already does.\n   297\t\n   298\t## 6. What it does not do\n   299\t\n   300\t- **No durability across process restart.** The log is one atom; the process dying takes it. A restarted host sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n   301\t- **No eviction, no `gap`, no `full`, no capacity key** — and a `:dao.stream.memory-log/…` key in a creation spec is rejected rather than ignored, so a caller cannot quietly ask for one.\n   302\t- **No attachments**: no `attach!`, no attachment identities, no frozen tails, no `:dao.stream/closed` from `cursor`.\n   303\t- **No encoding.** Values are held by reference, so this is not a serialization boundary; a live handle can pass through it, and no value is uncarriable.\n   304\t- **No size, depth, or retention query**, and no `closed?` predicate.\n   305\t- **No readiness or waiter mechanism** — the contract has none.\n   306\t- **No rotation, and no support for it** (§3).\n   307\t\n   308\tA later **durable** transport is a different transport, not this one grown. It would add: a medium that survives restart, and therefore an identity recovered from the medium rather than minted at creation; `attach!` resolving against the filesystem rather than a host directory; `:dao.stream/transport-error` on `cursor`, `next` and `append!` — a durable read is exactly a failure an operation can observe and return from, and locating the earliest retained position on a durable log is itself such a read (`dao.stream.md:483-488`); `:dao.stream/invalid-value`, because it encodes and is therefore a serialization boundary; a real `:dao.stream/full`, because a disk is genuinely bounded; and a `close!` that flushes, hence a named channel where flush failure surfaces (`dao.stream.md:583-586`). Nothing in `dao.stream.memory-log` should be shaped to anticipate any of that.\n   309\t\n   310\t## 7. Phasing and the handoff\n   311\t\n   312\t**Phase A, two commits.**\n   313\t\n   314\t1. `docs(stream): distinguish observable exhaustion from fatal host failure` — the *Complete history* amendment already sitting uncommitted in the working tree (`dao.stream.md:729-737`).\n   315\t2. `add the memory-log complete-retention transport` — `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/memory_log_test.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/retention_falsification_test.cljc`, and, in `test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc`, `run-retention-laws` plus the exclusion principle in the namespace documentation. `dao.stream.ringbuffer` is untouched.\n   316\t\n   317\tNo new design doc: the namespace docstring and the manifest are this transport's specification, and `dao.stream.file.md` documents a far larger thing.\n   318\t\n   319\t**Phase B.** The transactor plan is revised against it. The sentence it can quote:\n   320\t\n   321\t> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n   322\t\n   323\tOwnership is preserved deliberately: `dao.space` does not create its local stream and must not start. What that plan must **not** ask of the transport: durability across restart, a size or retention query, a `closed?` predicate, a capacity, or rotation — which §3 records as unavailable, not as delegated.\n   324\t\n   325\t## Verification\n   326\t\n   327\t- `bb test:clj` — JVM: conformance suite including the new retention laws, the falsification test, the totality tests, the creation-spec rule, and the concurrent linearizability histories.\n   328\t- `bb test:cljs` — Node; confirm `Testing dao.stream.memory-log-test` and `Testing dao.stream.retention-falsification-test` both appear in the output, since the shadow `:node-test` build discovers namespaces rather than reading an allowlist.\n   329\t- `bb test:cljd` — Dart; the same, minus the thread-based block. The negative and beyond-tail position tests carry no host conditional and must run on all three.\n   330\t- Regression: `dao.stream.ringbuffer-test` must pass unchanged — `run-retention-laws` runs only for a manifest carrying `:retention`, and the real ring manifest carries none.\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.stream.md b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\nindex 2b09e1f..38417b3 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n@@ -726,9 +726,15 @@ returns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n and evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n given up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n logically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n-`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite: unexpected exhaustion of\n-the underlying medium is `transport-error`, never the eviction of acknowledged\n-history.\n+`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite, and the line runs\n+between failures an operation can observe and return from and those it cannot:\n+unexpected exhaustion the transport can observe and return from is\n+`transport-error`, while fatal host or runtime exhaustion, from which the\n+operation produces no result at all, lies outside the outcome algebra\n+altogether. Neither is ever converted into the eviction of acknowledged\n+history. A transport whose every operation either completes its state\n+transition or does not return therefore has no firing condition for\n+`transport-error` and excludes it like any 